  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 300 AM MDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nUpper-level ridging is expected to persist across the Central Plains \nMonday through Wednesday. Confidence is high on southwest flow \nduring this period. High pressure with a plethora of dry air aloft \nis likely. A shortwave trough, accompanied by weak low pressure at \nthe surface, is forecasted to influence the County Warning Area \n(CWA) Monday into Tuesday. Dry air aloft is expected so nothing is \nto come from this feature other than a wind shift. Looking ahead, \nguidance foresees the next synoptic system making its way across the \nsouthwestern US Thursday. This negatively tilted trough should begin \nto influence the area then, keeping the 300-500mb winds \nsouthwesterly. Moisture advection is added into the mix with \ndewpoints anticipated to increase into the upper 50s for various \nlocations. Strong low pressure and a stationary front are forecasted \nto form at the surface, providing rising motion needed for rain to \ndevelop. \n\nIt will likely be quiet initially, precipitation wise, for a \nmajority of the long-term period as dry air aloft dominates. PoPs \n(Probability of Precipitation) guidance displays less than 10% \nthrough Wednesday evening. Add Thursday into the mix and rainfall \nchances increase a bit. Confidence on widespread precip is on the \nlow end right now as ensemble guidance attempts to project how the \nsystem evolves across the CONUS through Thursday. Nonetheless, a \ngeneral 25-35% is forecasted for the time being. \n\nA warmup is expected for the region as temperatures bounce back, \nwith help from high pressure, winds aloft, and plentiful sunshine. \nHighs in the upper 60s/low 70s Monday rise into the upper 70s/low \n80s Tuesday, and should culminate into more consistent 80s \nthroughout the CWA on Wednesday. Thursday should see a slight \ndecrease in high temps but mid 70s to low 80s is still forecasted \nacross the board. Overnight lows should sit in the 40s for Monday \nand Tuesday mornings then rise up to the 50s Wednesday and Thursday \nmorning. \n\nFire weather projects to be mild for this particular long-term \nforecast period. However, conditions will still have to be monitored \nthoroughly with relative humidity (RH) remaining low (20-30%), winds \nremaining gusty (up to 45 mph), and increasingly dry fuels expected \nacross the CWA through next week. RH values are currently forecasted \n20-25% Monday-Wednesday for portions of eastern Colorado. RH values \nelsewhere across the coverage area are projected at 30-50% through \nThursday. Transitioning to surface winds, Monday's winds are \nanticipated to be light and variable (5-15 mph from the North) \ngusting up to 20 mph. Tuesday and Wednesday project slightly \nstronger winds (10-20 mph from the South) gusting up to 25 mph. \nThursday is likely to observe the strongest southerly surface winds \nat around 15-30 mph, gusting up to 45 mph through some areas of \neastern Colorado.",
